1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,033
After a crash, the first job is documentation. Which cars, which
damage, which road, and what happened. ClaimScene turns accident
photos into a case a claims handler can trust. Illustrated, and
never faked.

2
00:00:13,033 --> 00:00:22,767
Generative video made accident scenes easy to fake and hard to
trust. A convincing AI reconstruction that is really just a
guess is dangerous as evidence.

3
00:00:22,767 --> 00:00:35,100
So the app explains itself first. Six short steps, opt in from
the hero, make the whole argument: what the drawing is, what the
AI picture is not, and what sealed does not mean.

4
00:00:35,100 --> 00:00:49,200
Every case starts with photos, yours or a sample scenario. A
vision model reads them into a closed vocabulary only: vehicle
kind, colour, approach, and a twelve position clock for damage
and impact.

5
00:00:49,200 --> 00:01:01,533
The proposed scene is yours to correct. Drag a vehicle between
the four approaches, or its handle around the twelve clock
positions, and the schematic redraws live. Confirming is not
verifying.

6
00:01:01,533 --> 00:01:13,300
Before you commit to waiting, the app says how long, and how
many real renders that came from. One click then submits a
background job it polls, because a live render takes minutes.

7
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:27,900
Both layers sit sealed, side by side. The schematic on the left
is the reconstruction, drawn by code. The clip on the right
carries visual style only, never the facts. And when the AI
picture fails, the app says so plainly.

8
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:40,733
Underneath, it is ports and adapters. A vision ladder reads the
photos, code draws the schematic to the same bytes every run,
Backblaze B2 holds the evidence, and Genblaze animates our own
raster.

9
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:52,467
Storage is Backblaze B2, content addressed. Every input photo,
every artifact and the manifest lands under a key that is its
own hash, and the seal records where each photo came from.

10
00:01:52,467 --> 00:02:00,000
The sealed manifest states that split in plain language: the
model supplies style, and nothing provable is left to it.

11
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:10,533
Press Verify, and the SHA-256 seal recomputes right in your
browser. Behind it sits a receipt of eighteen named checks, each
re-run from the stored bytes on the server.

12
00:02:10,533 --> 00:02:20,967
And the case id lives in the address bar, so a refresh or a lost
tab no longer loses the case. A sealed case is a link that still
opens next week.

13
00:02:20,967 --> 00:02:31,433
The accuracy is measured, not asserted: one hundred percent
weighted field accuracy on a committed seven scenario evaluation
set, with a dated scoreboard in the repo.

14
00:02:31,433 --> 00:02:41,167
Over eight hundred automated tests, secret scanning and a scored
readiness gate run in CI on every change. The live health
endpoint names the backends that served you.

15
00:02:41,167 --> 00:02:52,600
ClaimScene is live on Cloud Run, built on Genblaze and Backblaze
B2, and it is all open source. Honest accident documentation.
Illustrated, and never faked.
